WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.056
NARRATOR: Phishing is not defined by bad
spelling or email alone.

2
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:09.056
NARRATOR: It is a social-engineering attack
that uses a message to trigger an action

3
00:00:09.056 --> 00:00:10.484
useful to an attacker.

4
00:00:10.484 --> 00:00:16.401
NARRATOR: That action might reveal a password,
approve a sign-in, open malware, change

5
00:00:16.401 --> 00:00:19.132
payment details, or disclose sensitive
information.

6
00:00:19.132 --> 00:00:24.797
NARRATOR: Messages can arrive through email,
text, social platforms, collaboration tools,

7
00:00:24.797 --> 00:00:27.372
phone calls, or QR codes.

8
00:00:27.372 --> 00:00:34.117
NARRATOR: The attacker's advantage is context:
deadlines, authority, fear, curiosity, and

9
00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:35.956
routine business processes.

10
00:00:35.956 --> 00:00:41.021
NARRATOR: This lesson builds a repeatable
defense workflow: recognize suspicious

11
00:00:41.021 --> 00:00:46.086
signals, inspect destinations safely, verify
through an independent channel, report

12
00:00:46.086 --> 00:00:51.658
quickly, protect accounts, and respond
correctly if an action already occurred.

13
00:00:51.658 --> 00:00:54.017
NARRATOR: The goal is not perfect intuition.

14
00:00:54.017 --> 00:00:56.913
NARRATOR: It is a dependable process under
pressure.

15
00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:02.898
NARRATOR: Warning signs become useful when
treated as reasons to pause, not as proof by

16
00:01:02.898 --> 00:01:03.288
themselves.

17
00:01:03.288 --> 00:01:09.828
NARRATOR: The message creates unusual urgency,
asks for secrecy, invokes a senior leader,

18
00:01:09.828 --> 00:01:15.866
promises an unexpected benefit, threatens an
immediate consequence, or changes a normal

19
00:01:15.866 --> 00:01:16.872
payment process.

20
00:01:16.872 --> 00:01:22.670
NARRATOR: It may request a password,
multifactor code, gift card, bank change,

21
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.086
document access, or software installation.

22
00:01:25.086 --> 00:01:30.736
NARRATOR: A familiar logo or accurate personal
detail does not make the message safe;

23
00:01:30.736 --> 00:01:33.964
attackers can copy branding and use public
information.

24
00:01:33.964 --> 00:01:37.688
NARRATOR: Likewise, a legitimate message can
contain a typo.

25
00:01:37.688 --> 00:01:43.173
NARRATOR: Evaluate the requested action,
context, and verification path together.

26
00:01:43.173 --> 00:01:47.599
NARRATOR: The higher the consequence, the
stronger the verification should be.

27
00:01:47.714 --> 00:01:50.138
NARRATOR: A display name is only a label.

28
00:01:50.138 --> 00:01:55.063
NARRATOR: Expand the sender information and
inspect the complete address, including the

29
00:01:55.063 --> 00:01:57.115
domain after the at sign.

30
00:01:57.115 --> 00:02:01.690
NARRATOR: Look for look-alike spelling,
unexpected consumer email services, or a

31
00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.602
reply-to address that differs from the sender.

32
00:02:04.602 --> 00:02:08.850
NARRATOR: For links, the visible text may not
be the destination.

33
00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:14.109
NARRATOR: On a desktop, preview the
destination without opening it; on a phone,

34
00:02:14.109 --> 00:02:17.345
use the platform's safe link-preview behavior
if available.

35
00:02:17.345 --> 00:02:21.758
NARRATOR: Read the registered domain from
right to left before the first slash.

36
00:02:21.758 --> 00:02:26.967
NARRATOR: Attackers can place a trusted name
in a subdomain or path while the actual domain

37
00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:27.944
belongs to them.

38
00:02:27.944 --> 00:02:33.110
NARRATOR: Do not paste a suspicious link into
a browser merely to see what happens.

39
00:02:33.242 --> 00:02:38.102
NARRATOR: Independent verification breaks the
attacker's control of the conversation.

40
00:02:38.102 --> 00:02:42.835
NARRATOR: If a message asks for a payment
change, call the requester using a number

41
00:02:42.835 --> 00:02:44.097
already in your records.

42
00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:49.599
NARRATOR: If it claims to be an account alert,
open the official application or type the

43
00:02:49.599 --> 00:02:52.694
organization's known address instead of
following the embedded link.

44
00:02:52.694 --> 00:02:57.603
NARRATOR: If a colleague requests sensitive
data, contact them through a separate

45
00:02:57.603 --> 00:02:58.421
established channel.

46
00:02:58.421 --> 00:03:03.923
NARRATOR: Do not rely on a phone number, reply
address, or support link included in the

47
00:03:03.923 --> 00:03:07.362
suspicious message, because those can route
back to the attacker.

48
00:03:07.362 --> 00:03:12.146
NARRATOR: Verification should confirm both
identity and requested action.

49
00:03:12.146 --> 00:03:17.848
NARRATOR: Asking, 'Did you send this?' is
weaker than confirming the specific file,

50
00:03:17.848 --> 00:03:20.041
amount, destination, and business purpose.

51
00:03:20.186 --> 00:03:23.963
NARRATOR: Links can lead to
credential-harvesting sites or exploit

52
00:03:23.963 --> 00:03:24.383
attempts.

53
00:03:24.383 --> 00:03:29.817
NARRATOR: Attachments can contain malicious
code, active content, or deceptive forms.

54
00:03:29.817 --> 00:03:35.263
NARRATOR: QR codes hide their destination from
normal visual inspection and can move the

55
00:03:35.263 --> 00:03:37.597
interaction onto a less protected phone.

56
00:03:37.597 --> 00:03:40.518
NARRATOR: The safe response is not to
experiment.

57
00:03:40.518 --> 00:03:45.346
NARRATOR: Report the item through your
organization's approved mechanism and let

58
00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:47.980
security tools or staff analyze it.

59
00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:53.959
NARRATOR: If the document is genuinely needed,
ask the sender to provide it through the

60
00:03:53.959 --> 00:03:55.952
normal approved repository after verification.

61
00:03:55.952 --> 00:04:02.364
NARRATOR: File extensions and icons can be
misleading, and cloud-hosted files are not

62
00:04:02.364 --> 00:04:03.350
automatically safe.

63
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:07.635
NARRATOR: Treat an unexpected delivery method
as part of the risk signal.

64
00:04:07.778 --> 00:04:13.697
NARRATOR: Credentials should be entered only
into a known, verified sign-in flow.

65
00:04:13.697 --> 00:04:19.182
NARRATOR: A message asking you to reply with a
password or multifactor code is unsafe.

66
00:04:19.182 --> 00:04:24.571
NARRATOR: A realistic fake sign-in page may
capture the password and immediately ask for

67
00:04:24.571 --> 00:04:27.651
the code, allowing an attacker to use both.

68
00:04:27.651 --> 00:04:31.069
NARRATOR: Unexpected push notifications are
also a warning.

69
00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:35.480
NARRATOR: Deny the request rather than
approving it to make the notifications stop,

70
00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.551
then change the password from a trusted device
and report the event.

71
00:04:39.551 --> 00:04:44.407
NARRATOR: Password managers add a useful
signal because they normally fill credentials

72
00:04:44.407 --> 00:04:46.835
only on the exact saved domain.

73
00:04:46.835 --> 00:04:52.515
NARRATOR: Phishing-resistant authentication
methods further reduce risk, but no single

74
00:04:52.515 --> 00:04:55.355
control replaces verification and reporting.

75
00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:01.199
NARRATOR: Deleting a suspicious message
protects only one inbox and may discard useful

76
00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:01.638
evidence.

77
00:05:01.638 --> 00:05:05.959
NARRATOR: Use the organization's
phishing-report button, service desk, or

78
00:05:05.959 --> 00:05:08.360
documented security channel before deletion.

79
00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:13.042
NARRATOR: The original message contains
headers, routing information, URLs, and

80
00:05:13.042 --> 00:05:17.723
attachment identifiers that a screenshot or
manual forward may omit.

81
00:05:17.723 --> 00:05:22.862
NARRATOR: Reporting allows defenders to search
for related messages, block infrastructure,

82
00:05:22.862 --> 00:05:26.600
warn other recipients, and determine whether
anyone interacted.

83
00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:31.881
NARRATOR: Do not broadly forward the
suspicious content to colleagues as a warning;

84
00:05:31.881 --> 00:05:34.725
that can spread active links and attachments.

85
00:05:34.725 --> 00:05:39.783
NARRATOR: Follow the approved process, add
concise context about what looked suspicious

86
00:05:39.783 --> 00:05:44.840
and whether you clicked, then delete or
quarantine the item as instructed.

87
00:05:44.954 --> 00:05:50.514
NARRATOR: If you clicked, entered credentials,
approved a prompt, opened an attachment, or

88
00:05:50.514 --> 00:05:53.080
sent information, stop and report immediately.

89
00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:59.062
NARRATOR: Speed matters because defenders may
be able to revoke sessions, reset credentials,

90
00:05:59.062 --> 00:06:04.584
isolate a device, block transactions, or
remove malicious messages before the impact

91
00:06:04.584 --> 00:06:05.044
grows.

92
00:06:05.044 --> 00:06:08.003
NARRATOR: Use a trusted device and official
channel.

93
00:06:08.003 --> 00:06:13.296
NARRATOR: Describe exactly what happened,
including the time and information disclosed.

94
00:06:13.296 --> 00:06:18.603
NARRATOR: Change affected passwords through
the legitimate site and do not reuse the

95
00:06:18.603 --> 00:06:19.419
replacement elsewhere.

96
00:06:19.419 --> 00:06:24.734
NARRATOR: Follow organizational instructions
before powering off or altering a potentially

97
00:06:24.734 --> 00:06:27.633
compromised device, because evidence may
matter.

98
00:06:27.633 --> 00:06:30.771
NARRATOR: A fast honest report is a security
control.

99
00:06:30.771 --> 00:06:33.985
NARRATOR: Delayed disclosure gives the
attacker more time.

100
00:06:34.106 --> 00:06:39.451
NARRATOR: Organizations should not place the
full burden on individual recipients.

101
00:06:39.451 --> 00:06:43.951
NARRATOR: Secure email gateways,
domain-authentication controls, attachment

102
00:06:43.951 --> 00:06:49.094
analysis, URL protection, phishing-resistant
multifactor authentication, least privilege,

103
00:06:49.094 --> 00:06:55.522
logging, and rapid incident response reduce
both likelihood and impact.

104
00:06:55.522 --> 00:07:00.178
NARRATOR: Training should use the
organization's real reporting workflow and

105
00:07:00.178 --> 00:07:04.368
should measure constructive behavior,
especially timely reporting, rather than

106
00:07:04.368 --> 00:07:06.696
humiliating people who make mistakes.

107
00:07:06.696 --> 00:07:11.670
NARRATOR: Finance and account-change processes
need independent confirmation steps that

108
00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:15.152
cannot be bypassed by one urgent message.

109
00:07:15.152 --> 00:07:20.768
NARRATOR: Security teams need a way to remove
malicious mail at scale and communicate

110
00:07:20.768 --> 00:07:22.372
clearly during an incident.

111
00:07:22.372 --> 00:07:28.205
NARRATOR: Human judgment works best as one
layer in a system designed to expect

112
00:07:28.205 --> 00:07:28.622
deception.

113
00:07:28.754 --> 00:07:33.181
NARRATOR: Use one checklist whenever a message
requests a consequential action.

114
00:07:33.181 --> 00:07:34.941
NARRATOR: Pause before interacting.

115
00:07:34.941 --> 00:07:38.576
NARRATOR: Inspect the full sender identity and
destination.

116
00:07:38.576 --> 00:07:44.320
NARRATOR: Ask whether the request, timing,
tone, and delivery method fit the normal

117
00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:44.762
process.

118
00:07:44.762 --> 00:07:49.316
NARRATOR: Verify identity and action through a
separately obtained channel.

119
00:07:49.316 --> 00:07:54.550
NARRATOR: Report the original message using
the approved mechanism, then remove it as

120
00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:54.953
directed.

121
00:07:54.953 --> 00:08:00.196
NARRATOR: If you already interacted, report
immediately and follow containment steps.

122
00:08:00.196 --> 00:08:04.816
NARRATOR: None of these checks depends on
catching one spelling error or having perfect

123
00:08:04.816 --> 00:08:05.476
technical knowledge.

124
00:08:05.476 --> 00:08:10.434
NARRATOR: They create time and independent
evidence—the two things a social-engineering

125
00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:11.800
attack tries to remove.

126
00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.800
NARRATOR: A secure response is not 'I knew it
looked fake.'

127
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.800
NARRATOR: It is 'I followed a process that
would still work

128
00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.686
when the message looked real.'
